# nbdev authoring conventions

This library is authored in Jupyter notebooks under `nbs/` and exported to
`src/segmentation_tools_pt/` by nbdev. Notebooks are the source of truth; never
hand-edit files under `src/`.

## Environment
- Code runs on a Linux HPC via a Jupyter server. The local `.venv` holds Linux
  binaries and cannot be executed on Windows.
- To regenerate `src/` from notebooks without torch, use the Windows miniforge
  Python:
  `python -c "from nbdev.doclinks import nbdev_export; nbdev_export.__wrapped__()"`
- Run the runtime/torch test cells with `nbdev_test` on the Linux side.

## One notebook = one module
- Each notebook starts with a title markdown cell and `#| default_exp <module>`.
- Keep modules single-responsibility (see the plan's module map). A function does
  exactly one thing; compose small functions rather than writing large ones.

## Experiment-first cell order (per function)
1. Experiment cell: build a small synthetic sample (image/mask/tensor), run the
   raw logic inline, and print or plot the output to confirm it works.
2. Export cell (`#| export`): promote the working code into a function with a
   fastai-style docstring and a one-line usage example. Type every argument and
   add a short trailing comment explaining non-obvious parameters.
3. Test cell: assert the function's behavior on the synthetic sample. These cells
   double as the test suite under `nbdev_test`.

## Style
- Explicit imports at the top of the notebook.
- Generic by default: support multi-channel inputs and multi-category (multi-class)
  masks. Binary segmentation is the C=1 special case, not a separate code path.
- Notebooks must run with no external data: synthesize tiny samples. Provide a
  clearly-commented hook to point at real data paths.
- Write code that reads like natural language; prefer explicit over clever so the
  notebook is still understandable years later.
